{{/*
Every prompt defaults to the value already recorded for this machine, so
`chezmoi init --prompt` -- which re-asks everything, not just the one value you
came to change -- is non-destructive: press enter to keep what is there. Without
defaults, running it to set one key silently blanks every other, which quietly
drops an identity and its conditional gitconfig include.
*/}}
{{- $machineType := promptChoiceOnce . "machineType" "Machine type (personal/work)" (list "personal" "work") (or (get . "machineType") "personal") -}}
{{- $gitName := promptStringOnce . "gitName" "Git author name" (or (get . "gitName") "") -}}
{{/*
The email used when no identity's scope matches -- not necessarily a personal
address. On a work machine this should be the work email, so an unscoped repo
commits under the safer default rather than a personal one.
*/}}
{{- $defaultEmail := promptStringOnce . "defaultEmail" "Default email address (used when no identity scope matches)" (or (get . "defaultEmail") "") -}}

{{/*
Additional git identities are NOT prompted for and are NOT stored here. They
live in 1Password, one item each, in the vault this machine is configured for,
and `dotfiles-keys sync` writes them to ~/.config/git. A fixed slot per identity
could only ever hold one of each, and machine-local data would put a work email
on a personal machine. defaultEmail below is the default when none matches.
*/}}

{{/*
Personal machines use the 1Password desktop app to authenticate the CLI. Work
machines have CLI-only access through a read-only service account. On both
profiles, `dotfiles-keys load` streams the private keys into the OS ssh-agent;
the private halves are never written to disk. Work copies expire after a
bounded lifetime, while personal copies last until logout.

The backend is deliberately NOT stored in [data]. Anything written here only
appears after `chezmoi init`, so a machine that pulls a change and runs a plain
`chezmoi apply` would render against a config that predates the key. Templates
derive the backend from machineType instead, which every config already has.
*/}}

{{/*
No key material is prompted or stored. `dotfiles-keys sync` writes the public
halves to ~/.ssh from the vault, and the templates reference those paths rather
than embedding the keys. Reading them here via onepasswordRead was rejected
deliberately: every `chezmoi diff`, `status` and `apply` re-renders, so it would
prompt for biometrics on personal machines, break offline use, and make CI
unable to render without a vault.
*/}}

{{/*
Keys are scoped per machine, so this name ends up in the vault item titles and
in GitHub's key list. It is prompted rather than inferred: `.chezmoi.hostname`
resolves from the FQDN and can return something generic and network-dependent
(on the author's Mac it yields "app"), which would make two machines collide.
*/}}
{{- $machineName := promptStringOnce . "machineName" "Short name for this machine, used to scope its GitHub keys" (or (get . "machineName") (output "hostname" "-s" | trim)) -}}

{{/*
The vault this machine uses for dotfile credentials is its trust boundary. A
personal machine uses a vault only it can reach; it may also hold application
secrets such as Flow Icons. A work machine uses the vault shared with its
service account. That work vault may deliberately contain dedicated,
per-work-machine keys for a personal GitHub account; it must never contain the
personal machine's own reusable keys or unapproved personal secrets. Explicitly
work-approved application secrets such as Flow Icons may be copied into it,
because the service account can read every secret placed there.

Either a name or an ID works: dotfiles-keys resolves the name via `op vault get`
before ever building a secret reference, so characters like `<>` that a
reference would reject (verified against op 2.35.0) cause no trouble here.
*/}}
{{- $opVault := promptStringOnce . "opVault" "1Password vault for this machine's dotfile credentials (name or ID)" (or (get . "opVault") "") -}}

{{/*
Work machines read their service account token from the login keychain. The
service name is prompted rather than fixed, so a token already stored under
some other name does not have to be recreated just to match this repository.
Gated on machineType, not just documented as such: a personal machine
authenticates through the desktop app and has no use for this value, so it
should never be asked.
*/}}
{{- $opTokenKeychainService := "" -}}
{{- if eq $machineType "work" -}}
{{-   $opTokenKeychainService = promptStringOnce . "opTokenKeychainService" "Keychain service name holding the 1Password service account token" (or (get . "opTokenKeychainService") "dotfiles-op-service-account") -}}
{{- end -}}

[data]
  machineType = {{ $machineType | quote }}
  machineName = {{ $machineName | quote }}
  gitName = {{ $gitName | quote }}
  defaultEmail = {{ $defaultEmail | quote }}
  opVault = {{ $opVault | quote }}
  opTokenKeychainService = {{ $opTokenKeychainService | quote }}
